\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Two ways to use a model. In a workflow (a), code decides the order of the steps and the model fills in some of them. In an agent (b), the model decides which tool to use next, reads the result, and decides again, until it chooses to stop. Dashed arrows are possibilities, not a fixed path.}}{7}{figure.1.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:workflow-vs-agent}{{1.3}{7}{Two ways to use a model. In a workflow (a), code decides the order of the steps and the model fills in some of them. In an agent (b), the model decides which tool to use next, reads the result, and decides again, until it chooses to stop. Dashed arrows are possibilities, not a fixed path}{figure.1.3}{}}
